If you mean AWS Terraform and you give a key pair in the Terraform file, it depends on what you are giving.

Example
resource "aws_instance" "web" {
  ami           = "ami-xxxxxxxx"
  instance_type = "t2.micro"
  key_name      = "my-key"
}

Here:

key_name = "my-key"

does NOT create a new key.

It means Terraform will use the existing AWS EC2 Key Pair named my-key.

So:

Terraform
   |
   | key_name = "my-key"
   ↓
AWS
   |
   └── Looks for existing Key Pair: my-key

If my-key already exists → EC2 uses it.

If it does not exist → EC2 creation will fail.

If you want Terraform to create the key pair

You can create an AWS key pair resource:

resource "aws_key_pair" "mykey" {
  key_name   = "my-key"
  public_key = file("my-key.pub")
}

resource "aws_instance" "web" {
  ami           = "ami-xxxxxxxx"
  instance_type = "t2.micro"
  key_name      = aws_key_pair.mykey.key_name
}

Here Terraform creates/registers the AWS key pair using your public key.







key_name = aws_key_pair.mykey.key_name

the last .key_name means the attribute/property of the aws_key_pair resource.

Think of it as:

aws_key_pair.mykey.key_name
      │        │       │
      │        │       └── attribute
      │        └────────── resource name
      └────────────────── resource type

For example:

resource "aws_key_pair" "mykey" {
  key_name   = "my-key"
  public_key = file("my-key.pub")
}

Terraform understands:

aws_key_pair.mykey.key_name

as:

"Go to the mykey key-pair resource and get its key_name value."

So the result is:

aws_key_pair.mykey.key_name
              ↓
            "my-key"

Then EC2 gets:

key_name = "my-key"
What about .id?

If you write:

aws_key_pair.mykey.id

then .id means:

Get the ID of that resource.

For example:

aws_key_pair.mykey.id
          ↓
     key pair ID

So .id is not always the same as .key_name. .id is the resource's ID; .key_name is the key-pair name.

We commonly use .id when a different resource needs to refer to the first resource.
