Redis

Redis is an in-memory NoSQL data store. It stores data mainly in RAM, so it is extremely fast.
It can be used as a cache, database, message broker, and streaming engine.

Simple example

Suppose your application has:

User
  ↓
Application Pod
  ↓
Redis
  ↓
MySQL

Imagine a user logs in.

Without Redis:

Application → MySQL
              ↓
         Get user data

Every request may repeatedly query MySQL.

With Redis:

Application
     ↓
   Redis
     ↓
  User data

Redis can keep frequently used data in memory, so the application can retrieve it much faster.

Example

Store:

SET username Siva

Retrieve:

GET username

Result:

Siva

Redis uses key → value storage:

username → Siva
user:101 → {name: "Siva", age: 25}

The default Redis port is:

6379

Redis vs MySQL
MySQL	Redis
Relational database	NoSQL data store
Tables/rows/columns	Key/value and rich data structures
Mainly disk-based	Mainly memory-based
Good for permanent application data	Excellent for fast/temporary data
SQL queries	Redis commands
Port 3306	Port 6379
In Kubernetes

You could have:

Application Pod
      |
      | redis-service:6379
      ↓
Redis Pod

The application does not normally use the Redis Pod IP directly.

Instead:

Application Pod
      |
      ↓
redis-service
      |
      ↓
Redis Pod

For example:

apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: redis-service
spec:
  selector:
    app: redis
  ports:
    - port: 6379
      targetPort: 6379

Then the application can connect using:

redis-service:6379

So, just like your earlier MySQL example:

Application Pod
      |
      ↓
mysql-service:3306
      |
      ↓
MySQL Pod

Redis would be:

Application Pod
      |
      ↓
redis-service:6379
      |
      ↓
Redis Pod

Redis also supports data structures such as strings, hashes, lists, sets, sorted sets, and streams, which makes it useful for 
caching, sessions, queues, leaderboards, rate limiting, and real-time applications.


Application Deployment
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: application
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: application
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: application
    spec:
      containers:
        - name: application
          image: my-application:1.0
          ports:
            - containerPort: 8080
          env:
            - name: REDIS_HOST
              value: "redis-service"
            - name: REDIS_PORT
              value: "6379"


Redis Deployment
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: redis
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: redis
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: redis
    spec:
      containers:
        - name: redis
          image: redis:7
          ports:

Redis Service
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: redis-service
spec:
  selector:
    app: redis
  ports:
    - port: 6379
      targetPort: 6379

The Service selector:

selector:
  app: redis

finds the Redis Pod because the Redis Pod has:

labels:
  app: redis

Overall communication
┌─────────────────────────┐
│    Application Pod      │
│                         │
│  Application            │
│  REDIS_HOST=            │
│  redis-service          │
└────────────┬────────────┘
             │
             │ redis-service:6379
             ↓
┌─────────────────────────┐
│     Redis Service       │
│                         │
│     port: 6379          │
│     selector:           │
│       app: redis        │
└────────────┬────────────┘
             │
             ↓
┌─────────────────────────┐
│       Redis Pod         │
│                         │
│  label: app=redis       │
│  Redis listening :6379  │
└─────────────────────────┘

So the Application Pod doesn't need to know the Redis Pod IP.

